Fix the frame the simulated gimbal claims to report in.

PX4's simulated gimbal builds its attitude from the gimbal IMU in Gazebo:

    src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp, gimbalIMUCallback()
        _q_gimbal = q_ENU_to_NED * q_gimbal_FLU * q_FLU_to_FRD.inversed();

A Gazebo IMU reports orientation relative to the world, so that quaternion is
absolute. publishDeviceAttitude() then labels it:

        gimbal_att.device_flags = DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_VEHICLE_FRAME;

which tells every consumer the yaw is relative to the airframe. Anything that
believes the flag composes the value onto the vehicle attitude and applies that
attitude twice. Yaw the aircraft 90 degrees and the camera appears to swing
180. QGroundControl shows the gimbal turning twice as far as it does, and any
projection from the camera lands in the wrong place at any heading but zero.
Roll and pitch are affected the same way, and only yaw is obvious because
aircraft fly close to level.

This changes the label to match the data. It is the one line fix. The other
option is to make the data match the label, by subscribing to vehicle_attitude
in GZGimbal and dividing it out before publishing, which is more faithful to
the MAVLink gimbal protocol but a larger change.

READ THIS BEFORE YOU TRUST THE PATCH.

The consumer in this stack is MAVInsight. It builds d<N>_gimbal_frame_ref from
the LOCK bits of this same message (models/vehicle.py) and then applies the
reported quaternion to that frame (models/sensor.py). It reads no other flag.
DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_VEHICLE_FRAME is 32 and DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_EARTH_FRAME is
64, while the lock bits are 4, 8 and 16, so neither the stock label nor the one
below reaches that code.

GZGimbal sets no lock bit at all. MAVInsight therefore keeps the reference frame
equal to the body frame, and the absolute report is composed onto the vehicle
attitude a second time. So this patch removes the lie, and by itself it does not
correct the frame tree. To do that, the report must also carry ROLL_LOCK,
PITCH_LOCK and YAW_LOCK, which is what an earth referenced attitude means to
that reader.

Read from the code, not measured in flight. Check d<N>_gimbal_frame against the
vehicle heading before you trust either version.

    cd src/PX4-Autopilot
    git apply ../../patches/px4-gzgimbal-frame.patch

--- a/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp
+++ b/src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp
@@ -214,7 +214,11 @@ void GZGimbal::publishDeviceAttitude()
 
 	gimbal_att.target_system = 0; // Broadcast
 	gimbal_att.target_component = 0; // Broadcast
-	gimbal_att.device_flags = gimbal_device_attitude_status_s::DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_VEHICLE_FRAME;
+	// _q_gimbal comes from the gimbal IMU, which Gazebo reports relative to
+	// the world, so this attitude is absolute rather than vehicle relative.
+	// Labelling it as vehicle relative makes every consumer apply the vehicle
+	// attitude a second time.
+	gimbal_att.device_flags = gimbal_device_attitude_status_s::DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_EARTH_FRAME;
 	_q_gimbal.copyTo(gimbal_att.q);
 	gimbal_att.angular_velocity_x = _gimbal_rate[0];
 	gimbal_att.angular_velocity_y = _gimbal_rate[1];
